  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 221 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAs the aforementioned upper-level ridge shifts over the Lower Great \nLakes/Upper Ohio Valley Wednesday through the upcoming weekend, \nsouthwesterly flow will ensue over the Central Plains into Mid-\nMississippi River Valley with two distinct shortwave troughs \nnavigating this flow. Late Wednesday into Thursday, the first \nshortwave trough will pass over the Northern/Central Plains into \nUpper Midwest. Although ensemble model guidance probabilities of \nmeasurable rainfall are 20 to 30 percent in northeastern/central MO \nand west-central IL on Thursday, the strongest large-scale ascent \npassing well to the northwest, poor moisture return, and low \nconfidence in the attendant cold front even reaching the CWA favors \ncontinued predominantly dry conditions. Instead, the main impact of \nthis shortwave trough will be enhancement of WAA with temperatures \nwarming further above average through the end of the week. In fact, \nthere are 20 to 40 percent probabilities of 70+ F high temperatures \nin ensemble model guidance Friday and Saturday along/south of I-70.\n\nGlobal model guidance is in good agreement on the timing of the \nsecond shortwave trough through the central CONUS around Saturday, \nbut there is some slight variability on the exact equatorward extent \nof the trough axis and large-scale ascent toward the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley. With a stronger attendant cold front passing late \nSaturday and more time for moisture return, ensemble model \nprobabilities of measurable rainfall are much higher than with the \nprevious shortwave trough and at 60 to 80 percent (per 24 hours). \nThat being said, 0.5\"+ rainfall probabilities are 10 percent or \nless, suggesting that this rainfall, although welcomed, will not \nprovide substantial improvement in ongoing abnormally dry and \ndrought conditions. There is uncertainty in how much temperatures \ncool behind the front, with the NBM interquartile range spread 10 F \nfrom continued above average temperatures to temperatures near \naverage.",
